    "name":  "Chlamydial urethritis",
    "module":  "male-genital",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Chlamydial urethritis is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis (serovars D–K), an obligate intracellular bacterium and the most common bacterial sexually transmitted infection globally. In men it typically presents with mild dysuria and a scant, clear or mucoid urethral discharge, with an incubation period of 7–21 days. Up to 50% of infected men may be asymptomatic, making contact tracing essential. Untreated infection can cause epididymo-orchitis and, through female partners, pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility. First-line treatment is doxycycline (7 days) or azithromycin (1 g stat). Test-of-cure and partner notification are required.",
